umn `num_dependents` is dropped since it has low variance (only two unique values: 1 and 2) which might not provide significant information gain for generalizations.\ndf.drop(columns=[\'num_dependents\'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column `personal_status` is dropped\n# The column `personal_status` is vague and combines personal/marital status with gender information. It is better to break this into more targeted variables (e.g., male/female, married/single). Instead of dropping outright, we could initially conduct one-hot encoding for finer granularity in further steps if it proves useful.\ndf.drop(columns=[\'personal_status\'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column `residence_since` is dropped\n# While residence_since provides a numeric range (1-4), it offers limited variance and may not independently provide much insight about risk prediction. It can either be binned into categories or combined with other features (if justified), but dropped here for simplicity as a standalone feature.\ndf.drop(columns=[\'residence_since\'], inplace=True)\n```end', "```python\n# Step 1: Create a feature with ratio of credit_amount to duration\n# Feature name: credit_per_month\n# Feature description: The average amount of credit needed per month over the duration of the loan.\n# Usefulness: This feature is directly tied to affordability and individual financial burden. A higher value might indicate a higher financial strain per month and could help the model predict whether a person receives credit or not.\n\ndf['credit_per_month'] = df['credit_amount'] / df['duration']\n```end\n\n```python\n# Step 2: Transform 'installment_commitment' to reflect disposable income burden\n# Feature name: installment_to_income_burden\n# Feature description: The fraction of income spent on credit installment commitments based on the `installment_commitment` feature.\n# Usefulness: This feature illustrates the financial burden of the installment compared to the disposable income. A higher value may indicate whether the applicant is at financial risk.\n\n# Assuming median disposable income is approximated to a standard value (this could be refined with actual income data)\nmedian_disposable_income = 1000  \ndf['installment_to_income_burden'] = df['installment_commitment'] * (median_disposable_income / 100)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Step 3: Target encoding for 'purpose' based on the correlation with the target variable\n# Feature name: purpose_target_encoding\n# Feature description: Assign the average target value of credit approvals to each category in the 'purpose' feature.\n# Usefulness: Target encoding captures the implicit relationship between the purpose and the chances of being approved for credit. Essentially, some purposes might inherently carry more risk or credibility, influencing the prediction.\n\ndf['purpose_target_encoding'] = df.groupby('purpose')['target'].transform('mean')\n```end\n\n```python\n# Dropping irrelevant column: 'own_telephone'\n# Explanation: The 'own_telephone' feature has limited information value for this problem, as owning/having no telephone is unlikely to have direct predictive power in credit approval decisions.\n\ndf.drop(columns=['own_telephone'], inplace=True)\n```end"]
