# Feature name: duration_bin
# Feature description: Binned duration into categories such as short-term (4-12 months), medium-term (12-24 months), and long-term (greater than 24 months).
# Usefulness: Given that durations can have a wide range from 4 to 72 months, creating bins provides a categorical understanding of the duration, which could help a model better correlate credit duration with risk. Shorter-term loans may correlate with safer credit, while longer-term loans may have higher risk.

# Define bins and bin labels
bins = [4, 12, 24, 72]
labels = [short-term, medium-term, long-term]
df['duration_bin'] = pd.cut(df['duration'], bins=bins, labels=labels, right=False)
end

python
# Feature name: credit_per_month
# Feature description: Credit amount divided by duration (credit_amount / duration).
# Usefulness: This derived feature represents how much credit needs to be repaid per month. It could serve as a measure of financial burden on the borrower. A high credit_per_month value may indicate higher repayment stress, which increases the probability of default.

# Compute credit amount per month
df['credit_per_month'] = df['credit_amount'] / df['duration']
end

python
# Feature name: savings_employment_stability
# Feature description: Cross feature combining savings_status and employment (categorical concatenation).
# Usefulness: Combining savings_status and employment provides insight into a customer's financial stability from two perspectives: savings accumulated and employment duration. For example, having high savings and stable employment could signify low-risk customers, while low savings and unstable employment could indicate higher risk.

# Generate the cross feature
df['savings_employment_stability'] = df['savings_status'] + _ + df['employment']
end

python
# Explanation why the column num_dependents is dropped
# The column num_dependents is dropped since it has low variance (only two unique values: 1 and 2) which might not provide significant information gain for generalizations.
df.drop(columns=['num_dependents'], inplace=True)
end

python
# Explanation why the column personal_status is dropped
# The column personal_status is vague and combines personal/marital status with gender information. It is better to break this into more targeted variables (e.g., male/female, married/single). Instead of dropping outright, we could initially conduct one-hot encoding for finer granularity in further steps if it proves useful.
df.drop(columns=['personal_status'], inplace=True)
end

python
# Explanation why the column residence_since is dropped
# While residence_since provides a numeric range (1-4), it offers limited variance and may not independently provide much insight about risk prediction. It can either be binned into categories or combined with other features (if justified), but dropped here for simplicity as a standalone feature.
df.drop(columns=['residence_since'], inplace=True)
end
