2026-05-24 00:48:31,961 - INFO - thoughts: ['```python\n# Feature name: duration_bin\n# Feature description: Discretize the `duration` feature into bins such as "short-term", "medium-term", and "long-term" based on the duration\'s quartiles (e.g., Q1=12, Q3=24).\n# Usefulness: The duration of a loan may correlate with risk; shorter-term loans may correspond to lower risk compared to longer-term loans. Binning the duration can help non-linear models like decision trees better capture this relationship.\ndf[\'duration_bin\'] = pd.cut(df[\'duration\'], bins=[0, 12, 24, 72], labels=[\'short\', \'medium\', \'long\'])\n```end\n\n```python\n# Feature name: credit_per_month_zscore\n# Feature description: Apply z-score normalization to `credit_per_month`. Z-score = (value - mean) / std deviation.\n# Usefulness: By standardizing this feature, particularly for linear and distance-based models (e.g., logistic regression, kNN), we reduce the impact of scale differences and allow the model to focus on true underlying patterns rather than magnitude.\nfrom scipy.stats import zscore\ndf[\'credit_per_month_zscore\'] = zscore(df[\'credit_per_month\'])\n```end\n\n```python\n# Feature name: interaction_installment_credit_history\n# Feature description: Create a cross-feature between categorical `credit_history` and numerical `installment_commitment`.\n# Usefulness: This captures interactions between a person\'s credit history (e.g., "all paid" vs. "delayed previously") and their installment percentage of disposable income. For example, individuals with critical credit history but lower installment rates may behave differently than those with both high installments and bad credit history.\ndf[\'interaction_installment_credit_history\'] = df[\'installment_commitment\'] * df[\'credit_history\'].factorize()[0]\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column purpose_furniture/equipment_cross_savings is dropped\n# Many of the purpose and savings_status cross-features, including this one, have almost exclusively NaN values. This adds no value to the model and may even introduce data quality issues. Thus, it is better to remove this column.\ndf.drop(columns=[\'purpose_furniture/equipment_cross_savings\'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column purpose_new car_cross_savings is dropped\n# Similar to the previous cross-feature, the `purpose_new car_cross_savings` has an excessive amount of NaN values and does not contribute meaningful information for predictive tasks. Removing it improves dataset quality.\ndf.drop(columns=[\'purpose_new car_cross_savings\'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column savings_<100_cross_purpose is dropped\n# Though cross-features can sometimes capture nuanced relationships, this column contains only NaN values and does not provide sufficient signal for prediction. We drop this column to simplify the feature set.\ndf.drop(columns=[\'savings_<100_cross_purpose\'], inplace=True)\n```end', '```python\n# Step 1: credit_to_income_ratio\n# Feature description: Create a new feature that calculates the ratio of credit_per_month to installment_commitment. This represents the credit burden relative to the installment rate, providing insight into how manageable the credit is for the person based on their income allocation.\n# Usefulness: This feature can help capture nuanced financial stress indicators. For example, a high ratio might indicate a higher likelihood of financial strain, which is relevant for predicting credit risk.\n\ndf[\'credit_to_income_ratio\'] = df[\'credit_per_month\'] / df[\'installment_commitment\']\n```end\n\n```python\n# Step 2: log_credit_per_month\n# Feature description: Apply a log transformation to credit_per_month to account for its high variance and long-tail distribution. This transformation stabilizes variance, reduces the impact of outliers, and potentially improves the model\'s ability to generalize.\n# Usefulness: The log transformation often makes features more normally distributed. This can significantly help linear models or models sensitive to the scale/distribution of the input features.\n\ndf[\'log_credit_per_month\'] = np.log1p(df[\'credit_per_month\'])\n```end\n\n```python\n# Step 3: is_high_quality_job\n# Feature description: Create a binary feature that indicates whether the job is in the category "high qualif/self emp/mgmt."\n# Usefulness: Those with high-quality jobs may have improved creditworthiness due to more stable incomes. This feature directly connects socioeconomic status to credit behavior, adding real-world interpretability.\n\ndf[\'is_high_quality_job\'] = (df[\'job\'] == \'high qualif/self emp/mgmt\').astype(int)\n```end\n\n---\n\n```python\n# Dropping columns with excessive NaN values\n# Explanation why the column consists of many NaN values based on feature descriptions, and these columns provide redundant or no information (e.g., purpose_domestic appliance_cross_savings, purpose_education_cross_savings).\ncolumns_to_drop = [\n    \'purpose_domestic appliance_cross_savings\', \'purpose_education_cross_savings\', \n    \'purpose_furniture/equipment_cross_savings\', \'purpose_new car_cross_savings\',\n    \'purpose_other_cross_savings\', \'purpose_radio/tv_cross_savings\', \n    \'purpose_repairs_cross_savings\', \'purpose_retraining_cross_savings\', \n    \'purpose_used car_cross_savings\', \'savings_500<=X<1000_cross_purpose\', \n    \'savings_<100_cross_purpose\', \'savings_>=1000_cross_purpose\', \n    \'savings_no known savings_cross_purpose\'\n]\ndf.drop(columns=columns_to_drop, inplace=True)\n```end\n\n**Reasoning Behind Each Step**:\n1. **`credit_to_income_ratio`**: By combining two key financial indicators (credit and income percentage), the ratio encapsulates the relative financial burden faced by an individual. Those with higher ratios might be more prone to default. This feature captures an interaction effect without being explicitly present in the raw data.\n\n2. **`log_credit_