name: duration_binned\n# Feature description: Binned the `duration` column into three categories: "short-term", "medium-term", and "long-term" loans based on custom ranges.\n# Usefulness: Highlights group-worthiness beyond raw numerical values, which could improve the classifier\'s interpretability and performance.\n\ndef bin_duration(x):\n    if x <= 12:  # Short-term loans\n        return \'short-term\'\n    elif 12 < x <= 36:  # Medium-term loans\n        return \'medium-term\'\n    else:  # Long-term loans\n        return \'long-term\'\n\ndf[\'duration_binned\'] = df[\'duration\'].apply(bin_duration)\n```end\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Log Transformation of `credit_per_month`**\nReasoning: The feature `credit_per_month` likely contains outliers or a skewed distribution (as evident by its large range and significant standard deviation). Applying a log transformation will normalize this feature and reduce the influence of outliers, helping improve model stability.\n\n```python\n# Feature name: log_credit_per_month\n# Feature description: Log-transformed the `credit_per_month` feature to reduce skewness and normalize the data distribution.\n# Usefulness: Mitigates the impact of extreme values and ensures that the model treats the feature appropriately across its observed range.\n\nimport numpy as np\n\ndf[\'log_credit_per_month\'] = np.log1p(df[\'credit_per_month\'])\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Columns to Drop:\n\nThe "cross_savings" and "savings_cross_purpose" features appear to contain NaN values across all rows, and they do not provide insights for downstream predictions. These are redundant and should be removed.\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the columns with cross_savings and savings_cross_purpose are dropped\n# These columns are entirely NaN and do not contain any meaningful or actionable data. Dropping them will reduce dimensionality without losing information.\n\ndf.drop(columns=[\n    \'purpose_domestic appliance_cross_savings\', \n    \'purpose_education_cross_savings\', \n    \'purpose_furniture/equipment_cross_savings\', \n    \'purpose_new car_cross_savings\', \n    \'purpose_other_cross_savings\',\n    \'purpose_radio/tv_cross_savings\', \n    \'purpose_repairs_cross_savings\', \n    \'purpose_retraining_cross_savings\', \n    \'purpose_used car_cross_savings\',\n    \'savings_500<=X<1000_cross_purpose\',\n    \'savings_<100_cross_purpose\',\n    \'savings_>=1000_cross_purpose\',\n    \'savings_no known savings_cross_purpose\'\n], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Final Improved Dataset\n\nThese steps collectively include:\n1. Adding interaction features (`checking_credit_interaction`) for nuanced relationships.\n2. Binning continuous numeric values (`duration`) to create `duration_binned` for clearer segmentation.\n3. Log transformation on a skewed numeric feature (`credit_per_month`).\n\nThis should enhance the predictiveness of the model for determining whether someone is likely to receive credit.', '```python\n# Step 1: Generate new feature: `duration_bin`\n# Feature description: Bin the `duration` feature into categories such as short-term (<12 months), mid-term (12–24 months), and long-term (>24 months). This converts a continuous feature into a categorical one, which may better capture trends in how loan duration affects defaults.\n# Usefulness: Loan duration can influence credit decisions, as shorter or very long durations may correlate to either lower or higher risk. Binning helps capture these patterns more robustly and simplifies the relationship to the model.\n\ndf[\'duration_bin\'] = pd.cut(df[\'duration\'], bins=[0, 12, 24, 72], labels=[\'short-term\', \'mid-term\', \'long-term\'])\n```end\n\n```python\n# Step 2: Create an interaction term: `credit_history_cross_checking_status`\n# Feature description: Cross the `credit_history` and `checking_status` variables to capture the nuanced interaction between how someone’s existing checking balance interacts with their credit repayment history.\n# Usefulness: A person with a critical credit history but a strong checking account balance (or no checking balance) reflects a distinct behavioral subgroup, which could significantly influence creditworthiness prediction. Interaction terms like this often reveal compound effects that individual features may fail to capture.\n\ndf[\'credit_history_cross_checking_status\'] = df[\'credit_history\'] + "_" + df[\'checking_status\']\n```end\n\n```python\n# Step 3: Transform `credit_per_month` with z-score normalization\n# Feature description: Normalize the `credit_per_month` column to a z-score to standardize the range and remove potential issues from high variance.\n# Usefulness: The `credit_per_month` feature spans a wide range and has a high standard deviation, which might impair model performance. Standardization ensures this feature doesn\'t dominate others in models that are sensitive to scale, such as linear regression or KNN.\n\ndf[\'credit_per_month_zscore\'] = (df[\'credit_per_month\'] - df[\'credit_per_month\'].mean()) / df[\'credit_per_month\'].std()\n```end\n\n```python\n# Drop redundant interaction terms that are completely missing (`nan`)\n# Explanation why the column is dropped: The columns `purpose_*_cross_savings` and `savings_*_cross_purpose` appear to have all missing values. Since these add no information to the model and consume unnecessary memory and computational resources, it is optimal to drop them.\n\ncolumns_to_drop = [\n    \'purpose_domestic appliance_cross_savings\', \'purpose_education_cross_savings\',\n    \'purpose_furniture/equipment_cross_savings\', \'purpose_new car_cross_savings\',\n    \'purpose_other_cross_savings\', \'purpose_radio/tv_cross_savings\',\n    \'purpose_repairs_cross_savings\', \'purpose_retraining_cross_savings\',\n    \'purpose_used car_cross_savings\', \'savings_500<=X<1000_cross_purpose\',\n    \'savings_<100_cross_purpose\', \'savings_>=1000_cross_purpose\',\n    \'savings_no known savings_cross_purpose\'\n]\ndf.drop(columns=columns